Fixes #571

## Problem

`isJSONSerializable(null)` throws a `TypeError` instead of returning a result.

```ts
// src/utils.ts
const t = typeof value;
if (t === "string" || t === "number" || t === "boolean" || t === null) {
  return true;
}
// ...
if (value.buffer) { // throws TypeError when value is null
```

Two bugs on the same line:

1. Dead code: `typeof value` never returns `null`; it returns `"object"` for `null`, so `t === null` can never be true.
2. Throws on `null`: because the early return is never taken for `null`, execution reaches `value.buffer`, which throws `TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading 'buffer')`.

`null` is valid JSON (`JSON.stringify(null)` is `"null"`), so the function should return `true` for it.

## Fix

Replace `t === null` with `value === null`:

```ts
// Before
if (t === "string" || t === "number" || t === "boolean" || t === null) {

// After
if (t === "string" || t === "number" || t === "boolean" || value === null) {
```

This checks the value itself rather than its `typeof` result, so `null` hits the early `return true` before `value.buffer`.

## Tests

- New case in `test/index.test.ts`: `treats null as JSON serializable`, asserting `isJSONSerializable(null) === true`. Fails on `main` with the `TypeError`, passes with the fix.
- `pnpm vitest run`: 29 passed. `pnpm lint`: clean.

Branch is merged with current `main`. The related body-serialization case raised by @magion33 in the comments is a separate change and is intentionally not included here.

### 🔗 Linked issue

Fixes #559

### 📝 Description

The generic type `T` on `` call signatures was not being forwarded to `FetchOptions`. This caused `onResponse` callbacks to always receive `FetchResponse<any>` instead of `FetchResponse<T>`, even when the user explicitly specified the response type.

### Before

```ts
const instance = ofetch.create(defaultOptions)

instance<Data>("", {
  onResponse: context => {
    context.response // FetchResponse<any> ❌
  }
})
```

### After

```ts
const instance = ofetch.create(defaultOptions)

instance<Data>("", {
  onResponse: context => {
    context.response // FetchResponse<Data> ✅
  }
})
```

### Changes

In `src/types.ts`, changed `FetchOptions<R>` to `FetchOptions<R, T>` in both the call and raw signatures of the `` interface. This ensures the `T` type parameter flows through to `FetchHooks<T, R>` and ultimately to `FetchResponse<T>` in the `onResponse` callback.

